80
00:03:44,330 --> 00:03:46,490
to a problem you might
have already seen and maybe

81
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:51,060
solved mathematically,
which is pretty cool.

82
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:52,910
Obviously, to get
that, we're going

83
00:03:52,910 --> 00:03:54,710
to learn the Python
programming language.

84
00:03:54,710 --> 00:03:56,360
Once we get the
basics, we're going

85
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:59,600
to see how we can start
to structure our code

86
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,580
to look a little bit
better so we don't just

87
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:04,670
have a bunch of code
dumped in a file.

88
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,530
We're going to start
to organize our code

89
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:11,360
and see how we can make it
neat, readable, and modular.

90
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:14,750
And then towards the--

91
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:16,970
not in this lecture but
in a couple of lectures

92
00:04:16,970 --> 00:04:19,260
and as a theme
throughout this class,

93
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:20,959
we're going to look
