101
00:04:37,670 --> 00:04:40,160
the question, how do
we know the programs

102
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:42,070
we write are efficient?

103
00:04:42,070 --> 00:04:44,570
We can write programs, but how
do we know that they're fast,

104
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:47,328
and how do we know
that they don't take up

105
00:04:47,328 --> 00:04:48,620
all the memory in the computer?

106
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:51,710
So things like that,
comparing different algorithms

107
00:04:51,710 --> 00:04:55,670
that do the same thing
against each other.

108
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:58,330
So if there's no questions--

109
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:00,340
again, as I said, a
bunch of this information

110
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:02,470
is already in the
handout plus more--

111
00:05:02,470 --> 00:05:05,840
we can begin.

112
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:11,240
OK, so let's start by
talking about knowledge.

113
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:14,210
Declarative knowledge
is a statement of fact,

114
00:05:14,210 --> 00:05:17,420
and a lot of us probably
in math and in the past

115
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:19,250
have worked with
declarative knowledge.

116
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:21,300
But this is not how computer
science, this is not

117
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,570
how this class works.

118
00:05:23,570 --> 00:05:25,940
In computer science
what we do is we

119
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:28,790
work with imperative knowledge,
which is basically a recipe,

120
00:05:28,790 --> 00:05:30,540
how to do something.

121
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:33,050
And when we're programming,
all we're doing

122
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:35,870
is writing a recipe for the
computer to do something.

123
00:05:35,870 --> 00:05:37,920
That's it.

124
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:40,530
So here's a numerical example.

125
00:05:40,530 --> 00:05:44,210
The first statement is
a declarative statement.

126
00:05:44,210 --> 00:05:47,060
The square root of a number
x is y such that y times y

127
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:47,930
is equal to x.

128
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:50,150
There are many possible
values for x and y

129
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:53,600
that this statement
can be true, right?

130
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,880
But if we gave that
statement to a computer,

131
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:57,380
it wouldn't know
what to do with it.

132
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:00,770
What we need to do
is tell the computer

133
00:06:00,770 --> 00:06:03,440
how to find the square
root of a number

134
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:06,060
and then tell us what the
square root of that number is.

135
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:09,380
And so the computer
then needs a recipe.

136
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:11,300
So the recipe, a
really simple one

137
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:13,010
for finding the square
root of a number,

138
00:06:13,010 --> 00:06:15,500
is steps one, two, three.

139
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,540
So what we do is--
let's say we want

140
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:20,680
to find the square root of 16.

141
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,950
We obviously know it's four,
but the computer doesn't.

142
00:06:23,950 --> 00:06:25,530
And so we give it
an initial guess.

143
00:06:25,530 --> 00:06:27,680
Let's say the guess is three.

144
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,400
How do we go from there?

145
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:30,920
So the steps we follow--

146
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:35,960
step one, if 3 times 3,
9, is close enough to 16,

147
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,140
we can stop.

148
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:39,320
It's not really
close enough for me.

149
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:41,910
So let's keep going.

150
00:06:41,910 --> 00:06:44,810
Step two-- otherwise,
we're going

151
00:06:44,810 --> 00:06:46,940
to make a new guess
by averaging g,

152
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:50,180
which is our original
guess, 3, and x

153
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:52,280
over g, which is 16 over 3.

154
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:54,420
16 was the square root
we wanted to find.

155
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:58,210
So our next guess is 4.17.

156
00:06:58,210 --> 00:07:01,000
OK, using the new guess,
repeat the process

157
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,200
until we are close enough.

158
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:03,910
So we go back to step one.

159
00:07:03,910 --> 00:07:05,800
That's the first
part of the process.

160
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,210
We find guess squared.

161
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:10,390
4.17 squared is 17.36.

162
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:13,450
So now we say, is
that close enough?

163
00:07:13,450 --> 00:07:14,290
Not really.

164
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:14,830
It's not.

165
00:07:14,830 --> 00:07:15,650
It's 17.

166
00:07:15,650 --> 00:07:17,600
It's not really
even close to 16.

167
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:19,450
So let's do it again.

168
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:26,140
We make a new guess by averaging
4.17 and 16 divided by 4.17.

169
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:30,150
That gives us our
new guess, 4.0035.

170
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:34,210
OK, next step, using the new
guess, we repeat the process.

171
00:07:34,210 --> 00:07:38,430
So 4.0035 squared is 16.277--

172
00:07:38,430 --> 00:07:40,080
.0277.

173
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,720
Is that close enough to x?

174
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:44,490
Yeah, I could be
happy with this.

175
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:47,970
I could stop there because we're
within sort of plus/minus 1.

176
00:07:47,970 --> 00:07:49,300
So I'm OK with that.

177
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,320
But if we want it to be within
plus or minus 1 times 10

178
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:55,170
to the negative 6 or 7
or something like that,

179
00:07:55,170 --> 00:07:58,760
then we would
continue the process.

180
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:01,840
So really what we had
there is an algorithm.

181
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:03,608
It's a sequence of steps--

182
00:08:03,608 --> 00:08:04,900
step one, step two, step three.

183
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:07,450
There's some sort
of flow of control.

184
00:08:07,450 --> 00:08:12,070
We had a place where we said
if the guess is close enough,

185
00:08:12,070 --> 00:08:13,640
then we can stop.

186
00:08:13,640 --> 00:08:15,550
Otherwise, we do something else.

187
00:08:15,550 --> 00:08:17,710
We had another flow
of control where

188
00:08:17,710 --> 00:08:19,240
we said repeat this thing.

189
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:21,100
So we're kind of
not going linearly,

190
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:23,160
but we're changing the flow.

191
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:25,740
And then lastly
is a way to stop.

192
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:27,810
We don't want the
algorithm to go on forever.

193
00:08:27,810 --> 00:08:29,370
We would like to
stop at some point.

194
00:08:29,370 --> 00:08:32,429
And this stopping point, I
was kind of vague about it.

195
00:08:32,429 --> 00:08:35,940
But it could be when we
were within plus or minus 1

196
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:39,669
of the actual answer.

197
00:08:39,669 --> 00:08:44,680
And so recipes are
basically algorithms, right?

198
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:47,520
My grandmother was basically
teaching algorithms

199
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:52,020
when she would teach
me to bake a cake.

200
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:55,380
She didn't call it that,
but she was really.

201
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:58,410
And so even recipes have
that same structure.

202
00:08:58,410 --> 00:08:59,697
There's a sequence of steps.

203
00:08:59,697 --> 00:09:00,780
There's a flow of control.

204
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:03,000
Like, if you don't have
egg, use egg substitute.

205
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,690
Or repeat sticking
a toothpick to see

206
00:09:06,690 --> 00:09:09,520
if it comes out clean every
minute or something like that.

207
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:12,210
And then there's a way to stop.

208
00:09:12,210 --> 00:09:13,680
When the toothpick
comes out clean,

209
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:16,800
you take it out of the
oven, and you eat it.

210
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,700
And so computers are machines
that execute these algorithms.

211
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:22,190
They're actually dumb.

212
00:09:22,190 --> 00:09:24,240
Computers are not very smart.

213
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:26,220
They don't make
decisions on their own.

214
00:09:26,220 --> 00:09:28,490
They just follow these
sequences of steps

215
00:09:28,490 --> 00:09:30,440
that we told them to do.

216
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:35,000
Computers are good at storing
lots and lots of data.

217
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,680
We can't really do
that, but computers

218
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:40,520
can store gigabytes of
storage, terabytes even.

219
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:42,450
And computers can do
operations really,

220
00:09:42,450 --> 00:09:44,720
really quickly, which is
something we can't do.

221
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,170
They're good at
those two things,

222
00:09:46,170 --> 00:09:47,820
but they're not very smart.

223
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:49,880
They can't make
decisions unless they're

224
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:54,180
told to make the decisions.

225
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:59,240
So really, the computer only
does what you tell it to do.

226
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:02,830
And that's one of the big ideas
that I want you to come away

227
00:10:02,830 --> 00:10:05,080
from this lecture with.

228
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:07,420
Computer only does
what you tell it to do.

229
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:09,760
The sequences of steps
that you tell it to do,

230
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:12,520
that's the only
thing it follows.

231
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:15,810
So a little brief history
just to make you appreciate

232
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:19,320
programming, Python programming
language before we actually get

233
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:22,120
started with it is--

234
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:24,840
so before the 1940s,
we had these things

235
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:27,240
called fixed program computers.

236
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:29,280
A pocket calculator
as an example of that.

237
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:33,910
Every button was an operation.

238
00:10:33,910 --> 00:10:36,010
In the little screen,
you could use parentheses

239
00:10:36,010 --> 00:10:38,110
to put a bunch of different
operations together,

240
00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:41,500
but there was no way to store
all these operations together

241
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:46,227
to later put in different
inputs for that same sequence

242
00:10:46,227 --> 00:10:46,810
of operations.

243
00:10:46,810 --> 00:10:49,270
You had to input it every
single-- input those sequences

244
00:10:49,270 --> 00:10:52,980
of operations every single time.

245
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:57,810
After the 1940s, stored programs
computers came into play.

246
00:10:57,810 --> 00:11:03,600
And they were able to store
instructions to do things

247
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:06,390
as data.

248
00:11:06,390 --> 00:11:09,390
And there was a special program
called an interpreter that

249
00:11:09,390 --> 00:11:10,860
executed these instructions.

250
00:11:10,860 --> 00:11:14,370
It knew how to follow
simple sequences of steps.

251
00:11:14,370 --> 00:11:17,520
When the program told it to
go to a different location,

252
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:18,180
it did.

253
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:23,780
So it was basically
executing these instructions.

254
00:11:23,780 --> 00:11:28,730
And the instructions that it
did were arithmetic and logical,

255
00:11:28,730 --> 00:11:30,770
so addition, subtraction,
things like that;

256
00:11:30,770 --> 00:11:34,280
simple tests like checking for
equality between two values;

257
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,500
and moving data, so
taking this value

258
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:39,560
and putting it at a
different memory location.

259
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:41,860
So I just wanted to give
you a really brief overview,

260
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:44,410
and this is not super
accurate, but it gives you

261
00:11:44,410 --> 00:11:47,410
a sense of how
exactly things happen

262
00:11:47,410 --> 00:11:50,420
low level in the computer.

263
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:52,660
So the computer
basically has memory,

264
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:54,040
where things are stored.

265
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:57,730
It has an arithmetic logic
unit that does operations.

266
00:11:57,730 --> 00:12:00,130
It knows how to add things,
subtract things, multiply

267
00:12:00,130 --> 00:12:01,810
things, compare things.

268
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:03,970
And then it has the
control unit, where

269
00:12:03,970 --> 00:12:06,590
this program counter is set.

270
00:12:06,590 --> 00:12:08,610
And this is where
you put a program in.

271
00:12:08,610 --> 00:12:10,570
So let's see if this works.

272
00:12:10,570 --> 00:12:12,280
This is a program.

273
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:14,480
And up here is our memory.

274
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:18,250
So we have a bunch of memory
locations, 3456, 3457.

275
00:12:18,250 --> 00:12:20,500
And at each of these
memory locations,

276
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:24,750
we have some values
stored, prefilled.

277
00:12:24,750 --> 00:12:28,170
So when we first run this
program, what ends up happening

278
00:12:28,170 --> 00:12:31,260
is that the interpreter
sees the first instruction,

279
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:35,650
Add, the values at
3456 and 3457 together.

280
00:12:35,650 --> 00:12:38,190
So it goes to these
memory locations here,

281
00:12:38,190 --> 00:12:40,710
grabs the 3 and the
4, and sends them

282
00:12:40,710 --> 00:12:42,480
to the Arithmetic Logic Unit.

283
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:44,710
The ALU knows how
to do the addition.

284
00:12:44,710 --> 00:12:49,560
So it adds 3 plus 4, 7, and
sends the result back here.

285
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:52,710
Now, we never told it to
store that result anywhere.

286
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:54,930
But the next
instruction says Store

287
00:12:54,930 --> 00:12:57,270
the value you just
got back from the ALU

288
00:12:57,270 --> 00:12:59,760
at this memory location, 3458.

289
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:04,380
So the next step
basically takes that 7

290
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:08,320
and stores it at
memory location 3458.

291
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:12,650
Super tedious-- all we
did was add 3 plus 4.

292
00:13:12,650 --> 00:13:14,060
We do that again.

293
00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:18,020
We add the values
at 7889 and 7890.

294
00:13:18,020 --> 00:13:19,310
So it goes in the memory.

295
00:13:19,310 --> 00:13:23,410
It grabs the 5 and the
2, sends it to the ALU.

296
00:13:23,410 --> 00:13:26,710
The ALU calculates it
as 7, brings it back,

297
00:13:26,710 --> 00:13:32,270
and then we store
that in location 7891.

298
00:13:32,270 --> 00:13:34,970
And then after that, all
we've done is two additions.

299
00:13:34,970 --> 00:13:38,330
And then the next instruction
says Compare the values

300
00:13:38,330 --> 00:13:41,780
at memory locations
3458 and 7891.

301
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:45,670
So we're going to
compare the 7 with the 7.

302
00:13:45,670 --> 00:13:49,430
The ALU again does this
comparison and says,

303
00:13:49,430 --> 00:13:51,350
all right, well, 7
and 7 are equivalent.

304
00:13:51,350 --> 00:13:54,790
So this is true or whatever
it wants to give back

305
00:13:54,790 --> 00:13:56,860
to the interpreter.

306
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:59,140
And then the last
instruction here we

307
00:13:59,140 --> 00:14:01,820
have is Print the result
of that comparison.

308
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:04,930
So we print True
because they were equal.

309
00:14:04,930 --> 00:14:06,910
Again, super high
level, but it kind of

310
00:14:06,910 --> 00:14:10,160
gives you an appreciation
for programming languages

311
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:10,750
these days.

312
00:14:10,750 --> 00:14:14,260
This is very tedious to write
if we had to write programs

313
00:14:14,260 --> 00:14:16,470
in this manner.

314
00:14:16,470 --> 00:14:17,970
Alan Turing a long
time ago showed

315
00:14:17,970 --> 00:14:20,400
that you can compute
anything with actually

316
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:22,170
an even more basic
set of primitives,

317
00:14:22,170 --> 00:14:23,700
not addition, subtraction.

318
00:14:23,700 --> 00:14:26,640
But instead, with a
tape, you would actually

319
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:29,820
have six primitives-- move the
tape left, move the tape right,

320
00:14:29,820 --> 00:14:33,780
read the value at the tape,
put a value on the tape,

321
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:38,130
erase the value from the
tape, and no operation.

322
00:14:38,130 --> 00:14:43,790
And so since he showed this what
the result of it actually was

323
00:14:43,790 --> 00:14:46,760
is down here.

324
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:48,710
Anything computable
in one language

325
00:14:48,710 --> 00:14:50,820
is computable in any other
programming language.

326
00:14:50,820 --> 00:14:53,660
So if we had some
program written in Java,

327
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:57,530
that basically boils
down to something super

328
00:14:57,530 --> 00:15:02,540
long but something that is made
up of these six primitives.

329
00:15:02,540 --> 00:15:05,150
That means that if we boil
down this program to these six

330
00:15:05,150 --> 00:15:08,450
primitives, we can build
back up the same program

331
00:15:08,450 --> 00:15:10,370
in a completely
different language.

332
00:15:10,370 --> 00:15:11,660
And that's really powerful.

333
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:14,400
That's a really cool statement.

334
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:17,970
Now, we're not going to be
working with those primitives.

335
00:15:17,970 --> 00:15:21,630
We're going to be using the
Python primitives, which

336
00:15:21,630 --> 00:15:23,760
are more convenient,
and they allow

337
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:26,490
us to do a lot more
things in much less time.

338
00:15:26,490 --> 00:15:28,260
I'm going to do a
little comparison

339
00:15:28,260 --> 00:15:32,520
as we talk about the primitives
of Python with English.

340
00:15:32,520 --> 00:15:36,090
So in English, some of the
primitives might be words

341
00:15:36,090 --> 00:15:38,430
or even we can do
letters or characters.

342
00:15:38,430 --> 00:15:41,785
But we can say it's words.

343
00:15:41,785 --> 00:15:43,410
With characters, we
can build up words.

344
00:15:43,410 --> 00:15:44,993
With words, we can
build up sentences.

345
00:15:44,993 --> 00:15:46,710
With sentences, we
can build up stories.

346
00:15:46,710 --> 00:15:50,700
With stories, we can build up
books and things like that.

347
00:15:50,700 --> 00:15:52,740
In programming
languages, the primitives

348
00:15:52,740 --> 00:15:58,860
are numbers, sequences
of characters, operators

349
00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:04,500
like addition,
multiplication, division,

350
00:16:04,500 --> 00:16:07,020
checking for equality, checking
that something is greater

351
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:09,160
than, things like that.

352
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:12,210
So once we have these
primitives in a language,

353
00:16:12,210 --> 00:16:15,700
we can start to build up
the syntax of the language.

354
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:20,130
So in English, having something
like noun and noun and noun

355
00:16:20,130 --> 00:16:22,050
doesn't make any sense.

356
00:16:22,050 --> 00:16:23,980
Cat dog boy doesn't
make much sense.

357
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:26,050
It's not syntactically valid.

358
00:16:26,050 --> 00:16:31,100
But noun verb noun is
syntactically valid.

359
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:33,620
Similarly, in
programming languages,

360
00:16:33,620 --> 00:16:37,890
we can have two objects
kind of side by side.

361
00:16:37,890 --> 00:16:41,090
So here, this is a sequence
of characters h and i.

362
00:16:41,090 --> 00:16:43,670
And this is the number 5
right beside that sequence

363
00:16:43,670 --> 00:16:45,270
of characters.

364
00:16:45,270 --> 00:16:47,372
But that doesn't make
any sense, right?

365
00:16:47,372 --> 00:16:49,580
What does it mean to have
this sequence of characters

366
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:50,913
and that number right beside it?

367
00:16:50,913 --> 00:16:52,670
It has no meaning in Python.

368
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:54,980
Instead, what we
have to do is we

369
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:57,480
have to add an operator in
between these two objects.

370
00:16:57,480 --> 00:16:59,360
So here we add a
little star operator

371
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:01,550
in between the sequence
of characters "hi"

372
00:17:01,550 --> 00:17:02,990
and the number 5.

373
00:17:02,990 --> 00:17:05,119
And in Python, the
meaning to this

374
00:17:05,119 --> 00:17:08,300
is I want to repeat the
sequence of characters "hi,"

375
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:09,770
h-i, five times.

376
00:17:09,770 --> 00:17:13,410
So this would basically
give me hi, hi, hi, hi, hi.

377
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:17,280
So once we have
sentences in English

378
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:21,060
and expressions that
are syntactically valid,

379
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:24,280
we can now talk about the static
semantics of the language.

380
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:27,150
So in English, saying
something like "I are hungry"

381
00:17:27,150 --> 00:17:32,010
is syntactically correct,
but it's not static--

382
00:17:32,010 --> 00:17:37,590
it's not-- sorry, it doesn't
have good static semantics.

383
00:17:37,590 --> 00:17:41,640
There's no meaning-- there is
no meaning to that because the

384
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:44,850
"are" is for you or plural.

385
00:17:44,850 --> 00:17:46,620
Similarly, in
programming languages,

386
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:49,410
and this will differ depending
on what programming language

387
00:17:49,410 --> 00:17:51,000
you use--

388
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,070
here, in the previous
slide, we saw

389
00:17:53,070 --> 00:17:56,610
that you can use the star
operator between the sequence

390
00:17:56,610 --> 00:17:58,020
of characters and the number.

391
00:17:58,020 --> 00:18:01,690
And that meant repeat
that sequence many times.

392
00:18:01,690 --> 00:18:04,860
But if we use a plus operator
in between the sequence

393
00:18:04,860 --> 00:18:07,380
of characters and a
number, that doesn't

394
00:18:07,380 --> 00:18:10,410
have any meaning in Python.

395
00:18:10,410 --> 00:18:12,330
So it has a static
semantic error,

396
00:18:12,330 --> 00:18:14,700
even though it's
syntactically valid, right?

397
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:21,430
We have operator-- sorry,
object operator object.

398
00:18:21,430 --> 00:18:24,490
So, so far, we've been able
to find really nice parallels

399
00:18:24,490 --> 00:18:28,010
with English, the English
language and the programming

400
00:18:28,010 --> 00:18:28,510
languages.

401
00:18:28,510 --> 00:18:30,730
But this is kind of
where things break down,

402
00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:33,830
when we talk about the
semantics of a language.

403
00:18:33,830 --> 00:18:37,450
So in English, you can have
many different meanings.

404
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:41,680
The chicken is ready to eat
means let's eat this chicken.

405
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:44,320
Or the chicken is ready to
eat means the chicken wants

406
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:46,060
to eat something.

407
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:49,690
Programming languages, there
is no multiple meanings

408
00:18:49,690 --> 00:18:50,950
to a program that you write.

409
00:18:50,950 --> 00:18:53,680
Because the computer,
the machine,

410
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:58,000
the language follows the
set of instructions to a T,

411
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,870
there is no ambiguity
about what it needs to do.

412
00:19:01,870 --> 00:19:03,610
It just follows the
instructions and does

413
00:19:03,610 --> 00:19:08,350
what it needs to do to the
end, till it reaches the--

414
00:19:08,350 --> 00:19:10,060
it terminates the program.

415
00:19:10,060 --> 00:19:12,100
And so programs only
have one meaning,

416
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:14,080
but the problem is it
might not be the meaning

417
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:17,290
that you intended it to have.

418
00:19:17,290 --> 00:19:19,750
And that's when things
start to go wrong.

419
00:19:19,750 --> 00:19:24,000
We can have syntactic errors
in our program, spelling errors

420
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,840
and indentation errors,
things like that.

421
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:27,270
And those are easy to catch.

422
00:19:27,270 --> 00:19:31,830
Static semantic errors are
90% probably easy to catch.

423
00:19:31,830 --> 00:19:35,790
But the problem comes
in with the semantics.

424
00:19:35,790 --> 00:19:39,000
The meaning that you
intended this program to have

425
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,770
might not be what
it's actually doing.

426
00:19:41,770 --> 00:19:44,800
And that's where most
of my errors happen.

427
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:47,130
And that's where I get super
frustrated when I program.

428
00:19:47,130 --> 00:19:49,630
And that's probably where you
guys will get super frustrated

429
00:19:49,630 --> 00:19:51,600
too because you write a
program that you think

430
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:56,040
is doing one thing, but instead,
either it crashes right away,

431
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:59,520
or it runs forever and doesn't
really stop, or it terminates,

432
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:01,110
but it gives you an
incorrect answer.

433
00:20:01,110 --> 00:20:02,990
It's not what you
were expecting.

434
00:20:02,990 --> 00:20:08,550
And we'll talk about
this in a few lectures.

435
00:20:08,550 --> 00:20:10,400
So when we write
programs, we're basically

436
00:20:10,400 --> 00:20:14,120
writing sequences of
definitions and commands.

437
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:17,360
And we're going to write
these either in a file editor

438
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:18,770
or in a shell.

439
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:22,970
The first, today at least, we're
writing in the shell directly.

440
00:20:22,970 --> 00:20:26,360
And half of tomorrow,
we'll write in the shell

441
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:30,830
because we're not
really writing any--

442
00:20:30,830 --> 00:20:33,790
we're not really writing
many lines of code.

443
00:20:33,790 --> 00:20:34,790
We're just going to be--

444
00:20:34,790 --> 00:20:39,980
I'm just going to be showing
you some really quick things

445
00:20:39,980 --> 00:20:42,450
that we can do with the
Python programming language.

446
00:20:42,450 --> 00:20:45,740
So hopefully you all have
installed the programming

447
00:20:45,740 --> 00:20:47,480
environment.

448
00:20:47,480 --> 00:20:49,040
This is the Code Editor.

449
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:51,750
So tomorrow, we'll
start working in here.

450
00:20:51,750 --> 00:20:54,830
But for today, we're really
just going to work in the shell.

451
00:20:54,830 --> 00:20:57,815
And even in the
future, you can still

452
00:20:57,815 --> 00:20:58,940
type commands in the shell.

453
00:20:58,940 --> 00:21:01,730
I find the shell very useful if
there's just something really

454
00:21:01,730 --> 00:21:04,230
quick that I want
to check, that I

455
00:21:04,230 --> 00:21:06,817
don't want to write a
program for and then run.

456
00:21:06,817 --> 00:21:08,400
It's just like a
simple command that I

457
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:09,942
want to check to
make sure it's doing

458
00:21:09,942 --> 00:21:14,100
what I think it's doing before
I insert it in my code editor.

459
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:19,650
So here we have this.

460
00:21:19,650 --> 00:21:20,940
So mine is--

461
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:23,160
I guess I'm using the white
theme just because I find

462
00:21:23,160 --> 00:21:25,290
it easier for you guys to see.

463
00:21:25,290 --> 00:21:26,940
This is the file editor.

464
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:30,240
And this is just a bunch
of expressions or--

465
00:21:30,240 --> 00:21:32,940
yeah, a bunch of code that
we're going to type in today.

466
00:21:32,940 --> 00:21:36,137
And we're going to type it in
the shell today, so the thing

467
00:21:36,137 --> 00:21:37,095
on the right-hand side.

469
00:21:40,270 --> 00:21:45,310
OK, so what exactly do we
do when we write a program?

470
00:21:45,310 --> 00:21:48,220
At the base of it, we
are going to create

471
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:53,140
objects inside our programs, and
we're going to manipulate them.

472
00:21:53,140 --> 00:21:54,100
That's it.

473
00:21:54,100 --> 00:22:00,320
That's what programming is
mostly about at its core.

474
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:03,370
Now, when we create
objects, it's

475
00:22:03,370 --> 00:22:05,080
important-- this is
kind of something

476
00:22:05,080 --> 00:22:06,455
we're going to
come back to again

477
00:22:06,455 --> 00:22:08,770
and again in a more
high-level setting.

478
00:22:08,770 --> 00:22:11,440
But right now what I
want you to understand

479
00:22:11,440 --> 00:22:16,870
is that when we create an
object, an object has a type.

480
00:22:16,870 --> 00:22:20,590
And the type that an
object has tells Python

481
00:22:20,590 --> 00:22:23,870
the things you're allowed
to do with that object.

482
00:22:23,870 --> 00:22:25,400
So here are two examples.

483
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:29,100
The number 30, it's a number.

484
00:22:29,100 --> 00:22:31,460
The type we'll talk
about it in a bit.

485
00:22:31,460 --> 00:22:32,550
The type is an integer.

486
00:22:32,550 --> 00:22:33,763
It's a whole number.

487
00:22:33,763 --> 00:22:35,180
But basically,
what are the things

488
00:22:35,180 --> 00:22:37,580
we can do with this
integer, with this number?

489
00:22:37,580 --> 00:22:39,110
We can add it to another number.

490
00:22:39,110 --> 00:22:40,670
We can subtract it
to another number.

491
00:22:40,670 --> 00:22:43,110
We can take it to another power.

492
00:22:43,110 --> 00:22:47,510
We can take some other
number to this power of 30.

493
00:22:47,510 --> 00:22:50,240
A bunch of mathematical
operations,

494
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:52,260
as you would expect.

495
00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:53,940
So that's pretty
straightforward.

496
00:22:53,940 --> 00:22:57,770
What about this one here,
this quotation capital

497
00:22:57,770 --> 00:22:59,190
A, lowercase a--

498
00:22:59,190 --> 00:23:01,590
lowercase n,
lowercase a quotation?

499
00:23:01,590 --> 00:23:04,100
So this is something we'll
talk about next lecture.

500
00:23:04,100 --> 00:23:05,270
It's called a string.

501
00:23:05,270 --> 00:23:07,640
And it's a sequence
of characters.

502
00:23:07,640 --> 00:23:10,710
The quotations tell Python
it's a sequence of characters.

503
00:23:10,710 --> 00:23:13,550
And the characters part of it
are capital A, lowercase n,

504
00:23:13,550 --> 00:23:15,500
and lowercase a.

505
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:19,040
The kinds of things I
can do with this string

506
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:20,990
are not the same kinds
of things I'm allowed

507
00:23:20,990 --> 00:23:22,198
to do with the number, right?

508
00:23:22,198 --> 00:23:25,490
If I tried to take
Ana and divide it

509
00:23:25,490 --> 00:23:28,790
by the sequence
of characters Bob,

510
00:23:28,790 --> 00:23:31,340
Python would complain
very much because you

511
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:33,058
can't divide a string
by another string,

512
00:23:33,058 --> 00:23:34,100
a sequence of characters.

513
00:23:34,100 --> 00:23:35,630
It doesn't make
sense to divide it

514
00:23:35,630 --> 00:23:37,700
by another sequence
of characters.

515
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:40,790
Similarly, I can't
take Ana to some power.

516
00:23:40,790 --> 00:23:42,950
I can't multiply--

517
00:23:42,950 --> 00:23:45,420
I can't multiply by
itself, things like that.

518
00:23:45,420 --> 00:23:47,270
But the kinds of things
that I am allowed

519
00:23:47,270 --> 00:23:48,860
to do on a sequence
of characters

520
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:50,402
is different than
the kinds of things

521
00:23:50,402 --> 00:23:51,720
I'm allowed to do on a number.

522
00:23:51,720 --> 00:23:53,887
So the things I can do with
a sequence of characters

523
00:23:53,887 --> 00:23:56,240
is I can say, well,
what's the character

524
00:23:56,240 --> 00:23:58,010
at the first position?

525
00:23:58,010 --> 00:23:59,780
What's the middle character?

526
00:23:59,780 --> 00:24:02,090
How long is the
sequence of characters?

527
00:24:02,090 --> 00:24:04,460
How many characters do I have?

528
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:07,352
And so now you can see
that the type of the object

529
00:24:07,352 --> 00:24:08,560
is actually really important.

530
00:24:08,560 --> 00:24:11,950
Python uses it to know
the kinds of operations

531
00:24:11,950 --> 00:24:13,400
you're allowed to do with it.

532
00:24:13,400 --> 00:24:16,490
And so there's actually
scalar objects,

533
00:24:16,490 --> 00:24:19,930
and these are Python's
primitives, numbers

534
00:24:19,930 --> 00:24:21,670
and truth values.

535
00:24:21,670 --> 00:24:23,320
And there are nonscalar objects.

536
00:24:23,320 --> 00:24:24,920
We're not talking
about these yet.

537
00:24:24,920 --> 00:24:26,628
We'll talk about these
in a few lectures.

538
00:24:26,628 --> 00:24:28,570
But these have some
sort of structure.

539
00:24:28,570 --> 00:24:30,790
So for example,
a list of numbers

540
00:24:30,790 --> 00:24:33,862
has a structure because there's
a number at the beginning

541
00:24:33,862 --> 00:24:36,070
of the list, there's a number
at the end of the list,

542
00:24:36,070 --> 00:24:36,778
things like that.

543
00:24:36,778 --> 00:24:39,470
But a number itself
doesn't have a structure.

544
00:24:39,470 --> 00:24:42,410
It's just the number.

545
00:24:42,410 --> 00:24:44,912
So what are the types
of the scalar objects?

546
00:24:44,912 --> 00:24:46,870
What are the types of
the primitives in Python?

547
00:24:46,870 --> 00:24:52,660
Integers, so number 5, 0,
negative 100, 1 million.

548
00:24:52,660 --> 00:24:55,000
Float is another type.

549
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:58,870
It represents all the
real numbers, so 3.27.

550
00:24:58,870 --> 00:25:02,590
2.0 is a float because it
has a decimal number even

551
00:25:02,590 --> 00:25:04,790
though to us that just means 2.

552
00:25:04,790 --> 00:25:07,310
But to Python, if
you put in 2.0,

553
00:25:07,310 --> 00:25:09,020
it says that's a type float.

554
00:25:09,020 --> 00:25:12,350
Negative 3.14159,
things like that.

555
00:25:12,350 --> 00:25:13,850
Bool is a Boolean.

556
00:25:13,850 --> 00:25:15,620
It represents truth values.

557
00:25:15,620 --> 00:25:20,440
And there's only two possible
values that a Boolean type has,

558
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:21,470
True and False.

559
00:25:21,470 --> 00:25:26,690
And it has to be capital T
True and capital F False.

560
00:25:26,690 --> 00:25:31,670
And the last one is
this NoneType type.

561
00:25:31,670 --> 00:25:33,560
It's literally called NoneType.

562
00:25:33,560 --> 00:25:36,178
And it has only one
special value, None.

563
00:25:36,178 --> 00:25:37,970
We're not going to talk
about it for a bit,

564
00:25:37,970 --> 00:25:41,240
but we will sometime
in the future.

565
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:43,390
So to figure out the
type of an object

566
00:25:43,390 --> 00:25:47,360
when you create that object,
you use the type command.

567
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:50,440
So we can say something
like type parentheses.

568
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:51,670
And this is a command.

569
00:25:51,670 --> 00:25:54,048
And inside the parentheses,
you say, what do you

570
00:25:54,048 --> 00:25:55,090
want to find the type of?

571
00:25:55,090 --> 00:25:59,120
So if we do type of 7,
it tells me it's an int.

572
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:01,100
And if you want to do
the same command again,

573
00:26:01,100 --> 00:26:02,870
I hit the up arrow,
and it automatically

574
00:26:02,870 --> 00:26:04,580
puts in what I wrote previously.

575
00:26:04,580 --> 00:26:07,460
And then if I want
to do type of 0.0,

576
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:09,485
it's a float because
there's a decimal point.

578
00:26:13,110 --> 00:26:14,540
So this is basically
what I said.

579
00:26:14,540 --> 00:26:16,460
So we type this in the shell.

580
00:26:16,460 --> 00:26:23,290
And the shell tells
us what the output is.

581
00:26:23,290 --> 00:26:28,170
So just to reiterate,
int, float, bool,

582
00:26:28,170 --> 00:26:31,870
and NoneType are
types of objects.

583
00:26:31,870 --> 00:26:34,690
And there can be many
different objects

584
00:26:34,690 --> 00:26:36,850
you can create of that type.

585
00:26:36,850 --> 00:26:38,980
So if you think about
it, ints and floats,

586
00:26:38,980 --> 00:26:41,140
we basically have
an infinite number

587
00:26:41,140 --> 00:26:44,020
of objects we can
create of those types

588
00:26:44,020 --> 00:26:48,700
because we can have 0, 1, 2,
3, 100, 200, 300, 1 million,

589
00:26:48,700 --> 00:26:49,840
and all the negatives.

590
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:54,490
There's almost an infinite
number of values or objects

591
00:26:54,490 --> 00:26:56,830
that we can create of
type int and float.

592
00:26:56,830 --> 00:27:01,450
But bool, there's only two,
the truth values True or False.

593
00:27:01,450 --> 00:27:04,930
And the NoneType, there's
only one, this None.

594
00:27:04,930 --> 00:27:07,660
So that's the type, and these
are the possible values,

595
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:11,210
possible objects we can create.

596
00:27:11,210 --> 00:27:12,350
You try it.

597
00:27:12,350 --> 00:27:14,700
So you can just yell
out the answers.

598
00:27:14,700 --> 00:27:17,750
There's nothing to type unless
you want to check yourself so

599
00:27:17,750 --> 00:27:20,198
what is the type of 1234?

600
00:27:20,198 --> 00:27:21,540
AUDIENCE: Int.

601
00:27:21,540 --> 00:27:22,600
ANA BELL: Int.

602
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:24,730
Type of 8.99?

603
00:27:24,730 --> 00:27:25,510
Float.

604
00:27:25,510 --> 00:27:27,220
Type of 9.0?

605
00:27:27,220 --> 00:27:27,880
Float.

606
00:27:27,880 --> 00:27:29,780
Type of True?

607
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:30,280
Bool.

608
00:27:30,280 --> 00:27:31,720
And type of False?

609
00:27:31,720 --> 00:27:32,440
Bool.

610
00:27:32,440 --> 00:27:33,400
Perfect.

611
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:35,800
If you ever wonder what
the type of something is,

612
00:27:35,800 --> 00:27:37,425
you type it in here.

613
00:27:37,425 --> 00:27:38,650
You guys are doing well.

614
00:27:38,650 --> 00:27:39,700
Type is bool.

615
00:27:39,700 --> 00:27:43,870
Type of lowercase
t true is an error,

616
00:27:43,870 --> 00:27:46,390
just wanted to point that out
just to reiterate the fact

617
00:27:46,390 --> 00:27:49,000
that capitalization
matters in Python.

618
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,280
This is our first
error, by the way, guys.

619
00:27:51,280 --> 00:27:52,600
Very exciting.

620
00:27:52,600 --> 00:27:55,210
The error is a
NameError, and this is

621
00:27:55,210 --> 00:28:00,150
the message associated with it.

622
00:28:00,150 --> 00:28:04,490
You also know that it's
something special in Python

623
00:28:04,490 --> 00:28:06,500
when you have color-coded stuff.

624
00:28:06,500 --> 00:28:10,430
So you see capital T
True, capital F False are

625
00:28:10,430 --> 00:28:13,310
this dark blue here,
whereas anything

626
00:28:13,310 --> 00:28:17,900
that's not special in
Python is just black.

627
00:28:17,900 --> 00:28:19,910
So type is a special command.

628
00:28:19,910 --> 00:28:23,700
This is a float, so you
see they're color coded.

629
00:28:23,700 --> 00:28:25,110
OK.

630
00:28:25,110 --> 00:28:27,660
So once we create
objects, one thing

631
00:28:27,660 --> 00:28:30,300
we can do with these
objects is to cast them

632
00:28:30,300 --> 00:28:31,680
to a different type.

633
00:28:31,680 --> 00:28:36,300
Now, this is a little
bit maybe confusing

634
00:28:36,300 --> 00:28:39,660
because we're not actually
changing the object once we've

635
00:28:39,660 --> 00:28:40,720
created it.

636
00:28:40,720 --> 00:28:46,210
So once we create the integer
3, it's there in memory.

637
00:28:46,210 --> 00:28:49,900
If we cast that integer
to a float version of it,

638
00:28:49,900 --> 00:28:52,240
we're creating a new
object in memory.

639
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:53,290
We're not changing the 3.

640
00:28:53,290 --> 00:28:55,310
The 3 already exists.

641
00:28:55,310 --> 00:28:57,640
We're just getting the
float version of it

642
00:28:57,640 --> 00:29:00,440
and storing it as a
new object in memory.

643
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:03,790
So when we do float
3, this is a command

644
00:29:03,790 --> 00:29:08,140
that gets for me the float
version of the integer 3.

645
00:29:08,140 --> 00:29:10,720
So that will give me 3.0.

646
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:16,750
So for example, this
is what I had, float 3.

647
00:29:16,750 --> 00:29:18,970
The output is 3.0.

648
00:29:18,970 --> 00:29:26,120
If I do int of 5.2,
it truncates it,

649
00:29:26,120 --> 00:29:29,090
and it gives me the integer
portion of this float.

650
00:29:29,090 --> 00:29:33,582
If I do int of 5.9,
it still truncates it

651
00:29:33,582 --> 00:29:35,540
and gives me the integer
version of this float.

652
00:29:35,540 --> 00:29:37,670
It doesn't round.

653
00:29:37,670 --> 00:29:42,200
I'm just asking for the
integer version of this float.

654
00:29:42,200 --> 00:29:45,780
Some operations like
round is an operation

655
00:29:45,780 --> 00:29:48,870
we can do has an
implicit cast in it.

656
00:29:48,870 --> 00:29:53,570
So if I round 5.9, it's actually
going to round it to 6.0

657
00:29:53,570 --> 00:29:55,710
and then cast it to an integer.

658
00:29:55,710 --> 00:29:58,550
So notice it doesn't
give me as an output 6.0.

659
00:29:58,550 --> 00:30:00,620
It then rounds it to just six.

661
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:08,100
So that's basically what
I said in the example.

662
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:10,940
So let's have you try this.

663
00:30:10,940 --> 00:30:12,800
What are the types
of the following?

664
00:30:12,800 --> 00:30:14,820
I don't need the
values but the types.

665
00:30:14,820 --> 00:30:23,640
So if I get type of float of
123, what is the type of that?

666
00:30:23,640 --> 00:30:24,990
Float, yeah, exactly.

667
00:30:24,990 --> 00:30:26,850
Yep.

668
00:30:26,850 --> 00:30:28,830
What if I round 7.9?

669
00:30:28,830 --> 00:30:30,180
What's the type of the result?

670
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:31,080
Int, yep.

671
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:35,700
What if I create a float
of the round of 7.2?

672
00:30:35,700 --> 00:30:36,390
AUDIENCE: Float.

673
00:30:36,390 --> 00:30:37,920
ANA BELL: Yes, good.

674
00:30:37,920 --> 00:30:39,300
Float would be 7.0.

675
00:30:39,300 --> 00:30:41,550
And the int of 7.2?

676
00:30:41,550 --> 00:30:42,924
AUDIENCE: 7.

677
00:30:42,924 --> 00:30:43,840
Int.

678
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:45,040
ANA BELL: Int, yes, exactly.

679
00:30:45,040 --> 00:30:46,510
I want the type not the value.

680
00:30:46,510 --> 00:30:51,490
And the int of 7.9
is an int, exactly.

681
00:30:51,490 --> 00:30:54,060
Awesome, good.

682
00:30:54,060 --> 00:30:57,075
OK, so we've created
a bunch of objects.

683
00:30:57,075 --> 00:30:58,950
We know that we can
create a bunch of objects

684
00:30:58,950 --> 00:30:59,670
in our programs.

685
00:30:59,670 --> 00:31:01,180
What do we do with them?

686
00:31:01,180 --> 00:31:05,380
Well, we can combine
them into expressions.

687
00:31:05,380 --> 00:31:08,790
So let's say we have 3 plus 2.

688
00:31:08,790 --> 00:31:11,490
I've got object,
operator, object.

689
00:31:11,490 --> 00:31:13,530
Cool, syntactically
valid in Python

690
00:31:13,530 --> 00:31:15,910
and has no static
semantic error.

691
00:31:15,910 --> 00:31:21,120
So if I do that in Python,
it's going to be OK.

692
00:31:21,120 --> 00:31:25,100
3 plus 2, 5.

693
00:31:25,100 --> 00:31:31,420
And the type of 3
plus 2 is an integer.

694
00:31:31,420 --> 00:31:32,980
So basically what
I've done here,

695
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:38,320
I've put an expression
within this type command.

696
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:39,760
And that's OK.

697
00:31:39,760 --> 00:31:41,530
That's, in fact,
encouraged in Python.

698
00:31:41,530 --> 00:31:44,665
You don't just want to
calculate and then stick in.

699
00:31:44,665 --> 00:31:46,040
That would be
very, very tedious.

700
00:31:46,040 --> 00:31:50,360
So you can insert expressions
in many, many different places.

701
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:53,510
So here we have 3 plus
2, 5 divided by 3.

702
00:31:53,510 --> 00:31:56,630
Again, we've got 5 divided
by 3 has this decimal value.

703
00:31:56,630 --> 00:31:59,480
And the result has a float--

704
00:31:59,480 --> 00:32:01,830
is of type float.

705
00:32:01,830 --> 00:32:03,380
So the important
thing to remember

706
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:08,390
when we're doing expressions
is Python reads the expression,

707
00:32:08,390 --> 00:32:12,680
but it does not store
the expression in memory.

708
00:32:12,680 --> 00:32:15,080
What it does is it
reads the expression,

709
00:32:15,080 --> 00:32:18,330
evaluates it to
one single value,

710
00:32:18,330 --> 00:32:22,050
and then it stores the
result value in memory.

711
00:32:22,050 --> 00:32:23,690
So it never stores
the expression.

712
00:32:23,690 --> 00:32:27,650
It evaluates the expression
and then stores the value.

713
00:32:27,650 --> 00:32:30,680
And so this is the syntax for an
expression-- object, operator,

714
00:32:30,680 --> 00:32:33,180
object, as we just saw.

715
00:32:33,180 --> 00:32:36,650
And that's really-- and
the idea I said before,

716
00:32:36,650 --> 00:32:40,340
where Python stores
values of expressions,

717
00:32:40,340 --> 00:32:45,480
not the expressions themselves,
is really, really important.

718
00:32:45,480 --> 00:32:48,420
So this is my first
big idea slide.

719
00:32:48,420 --> 00:32:50,610
I decided to insert
these because I

720
00:32:50,610 --> 00:32:53,590
think they stress the
importance of several concepts.

721
00:32:53,590 --> 00:32:55,240
So I hope this is one.

722
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:57,690
So we're taking expressions.

723
00:32:57,690 --> 00:32:59,320
They can be as
complex as you'd like.

724
00:32:59,320 --> 00:33:01,860
We can use parentheses,
a bunch of-- it

725
00:33:01,860 --> 00:33:05,070
doesn't just have to be
object, operator, object.

726
00:33:05,070 --> 00:33:07,440
It can be more
complex than that.

727
00:33:07,440 --> 00:33:10,590
But basically, however
complex that expression

728
00:33:10,590 --> 00:33:14,730
is, we evaluate it, and we
replace it with one value.

729
00:33:14,730 --> 00:33:18,350
And the expression can
be something like this.

730
00:33:18,350 --> 00:33:20,920
It doesn't just have to be
something that's mathematical.

731
00:33:20,920 --> 00:33:22,660
This was a mathematical
expression,

732
00:33:22,660 --> 00:33:24,400
but this is also an expression.

733
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:25,660
And it evaluates.

734
00:33:25,660 --> 00:33:31,240
So this entire thing evaluates
to this word, this word which

735
00:33:31,240 --> 00:33:34,270
represents the type integer.

736
00:33:34,270 --> 00:33:36,660
So here are some more examples.

737
00:33:36,660 --> 00:33:39,480
3 plus 2, again.

738
00:33:39,480 --> 00:33:42,270
We've got these examples
with the parentheses,

739
00:33:42,270 --> 00:33:44,220
4 plus 2 times 6
minus 1 obviously

740
00:33:44,220 --> 00:33:46,140
gives us the number, 35.

741
00:33:46,140 --> 00:33:49,120
And then we can insert
expressions wherever we'd like.

742
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:51,930
So here I'm inserting
that specific expression

743
00:33:51,930 --> 00:33:54,220
in the type command.

744
00:33:54,220 --> 00:33:56,940
And this is also an
expression, like I just said.

745
00:33:56,940 --> 00:34:00,570
And its result is int.

746
00:34:00,570 --> 00:34:04,080
And similarly, we can also
insert that expression here.

747
00:34:04,080 --> 00:34:06,900
And then we can wrap
that around cast.

748
00:34:06,900 --> 00:34:08,489
And it gives us a float.

749
00:34:08,489 --> 00:34:09,796
Yes?

750
00:34:09,796 --> 00:34:11,949
AUDIENCE: So when you're
inserting expressions

751
00:34:11,949 --> 00:34:15,035
[INAUDIBLE] include
the operators--

752
00:34:15,035 --> 00:34:16,253
[INAUDIBLE] operators in?

753
00:34:16,253 --> 00:34:17,920
ANA BELL: When you're
inserting-- sorry,

754
00:34:17,920 --> 00:34:19,299
when you're inserting what?

755
00:34:19,299 --> 00:34:21,424
AUDIENCE: Well, since you
said they're expressions,

756
00:34:21,424 --> 00:34:24,550
and you said that you need
like object, operator, object,

