00:46:18,730 --> 00:46:21,810
And then I can invoke the
name of the variable I just

1013
00:46:21,810 --> 00:46:23,980
created simply by typing it in.

1014
00:46:23,980 --> 00:46:27,690
So if I type in
xy, it gives me 7.

1015
00:46:27,690 --> 00:46:32,250
And then I can do operations
with it, xy plus 1 is 8.

1016
00:46:32,250 --> 00:46:32,820
Yeah.

1017
00:46:32,820 --> 00:46:34,695
AUDIENCE: Before you
were putting the strings

1018
00:46:34,695 --> 00:46:35,760
with apostrophes.

1019
00:46:35,760 --> 00:46:37,420
So wouldn't you need that?

1020
00:46:37,420 --> 00:46:40,360
ANA BELL: So those are strings,
right, sequences of characters.

1021
00:46:40,360 --> 00:46:42,140
Here, these are variables.

1022
00:46:42,140 --> 00:46:47,280
So these are names that I
am giving as a variable.

1023
00:46:47,280 --> 00:46:48,530
Yeah, that's a great question.

1024
00:46:48,530 --> 00:46:51,010
So this is going to be a string.

1025
00:46:51,010 --> 00:46:52,840
And you notice it changed color.

1026
00:46:52,840 --> 00:46:55,390
It has some meaning in Python.

1027
00:46:55,390 --> 00:46:58,690
But xy is a variable
that I create.

1029
00:47:03,760 --> 00:47:08,080
OK, so why do we want to
give names to variables?

1030
00:47:08,080 --> 00:47:10,990
Because as I showed you
with the pi example,

1031
00:47:10,990 --> 00:47:14,530
it's a lot easier to
write readable code

1032
00:47:14,530 --> 00:47:18,470
if you have variable names
within your programs.

1033
00:47:18,470 --> 00:47:20,560
So when you grab--

1034
00:47:20,560 --> 00:47:23,110
when you write programs,
it's important to choose

1035
00:47:23,110 --> 00:47:25,060
variable names wisely.

1036
00:47:25,060 --> 00:47:27,472
You don't want to use
just single letters.

1037
00:47:27,472 --> 00:47:28,930
You don't want to
name it something

1038
00:47:28,930 --> 00:47:31,305
that doesn't have something
to do with the program you're

1039
00:47:31,305 --> 00:47:33,640
writing, because
you're going to want

1040
00:47:33,640 --> 00:47:36,940
to reread these programs
sometime in the future.

1041
00:47:36,940 --> 00:47:39,490
Or others might want to
read your programs sometime

1042
00:47:39,490 --> 00:47:40,730
in the future.

1043
00:47:40,730 --> 00:47:43,240
So here's an example
of a nice program.

1044
00:47:43,240 --> 00:47:46,120
It's just basically four
assignment statements

1045
00:47:46,120 --> 00:47:48,350
that do some calculations.

1046
00:47:48,350 --> 00:47:51,050
The first line of the
program is not really a line.

1047
00:47:51,050 --> 00:47:52,950
It's called a comment.

1048
00:47:52,950 --> 00:47:54,700
You can have as many
of these as you like.

1049
00:47:54,700 --> 00:47:56,020
They start with a hash.

1050
00:47:56,020 --> 00:47:57,520
It's a line that
starts with a hash.

1051
00:47:57,520 --> 00:48:00,880
And it's basically
a text that you

1052
00:48:00,880 --> 00:48:05,690
write that helps you or
others figure out what

1053
00:48:05,690 --> 00:48:07,020
the code is supposed to do.

1054
00:48:07,020 --> 00:48:10,430
And usually we comment large
chunks of code at a time,

1055
00:48:10,430 --> 00:48:13,700
not line by line.

1056
00:48:13,700 --> 00:48:17,790
Then we have these four
assignment statements.

1057
00:48:17,790 --> 00:48:22,160
So here I'm defining variable
named pi bound to the value

1058
00:48:22,160 --> 00:48:25,430
here, so not the
division but 3.14159.

1059
00:48:25,430 --> 00:48:28,820
Variable named radius
bound to this float 2.2.

1060
00:48:28,820 --> 00:48:30,740
And then I have a
variable named area

1061
00:48:30,740 --> 00:48:34,580
which is bound to the
result of this expression.

1062
00:48:34,580 --> 00:48:38,330
So when Python sees
my pi and my radius,

1063
00:48:38,330 --> 00:48:42,260
it grabs them from memory,
replaces them with the values,

1064
00:48:42,260 --> 00:48:45,680
evaluates the expression,
grabs that one

1065
00:48:45,680 --> 00:48:49,280
value that we evaluated to
15-point-something, whatever

1066
00:48:49,280 --> 00:48:52,760
this is, and binds the
15-point-something to the name

1067
00:48:52,760 --> 00:48:54,200
area.

1068
00:48:54,200 --> 00:48:55,760
Same with circumference.

1070
00:48:59,578 --> 00:49:01,370
Code style is something
that we're actually

1071
00:49:01,370 --> 00:49:03,270
going to look at in
your problem sets.

1072
00:49:03,270 --> 00:49:05,540
So I just wanted to
quickly talk about that.

1073
00:49:05,540 --> 00:49:09,560
Here is a program that
has really bad style.

1074
00:49:09,560 --> 00:49:10,940
Actually, that shouldn't be meh.

1075
00:49:10,940 --> 00:49:13,370
It should be terrible
or something like that.

1076
00:49:13,370 --> 00:49:15,830
But in case you
haven't noticed, it's

1077
00:49:15,830 --> 00:49:17,900
the same program as
on the previous slide.

1078
00:49:17,900 --> 00:49:20,600
But if I gave you this
program straight off the bat,

1079
00:49:20,600 --> 00:49:23,030
you probably wouldn't
know what it's doing.

1080
00:49:23,030 --> 00:49:27,020
It's reusing 355
over 113 twice here.

1081
00:49:27,020 --> 00:49:30,140
It's using just a and
c as variable names.

1082
00:49:30,140 --> 00:49:33,720
Its description is
"do calculations."

1083
00:49:33,720 --> 00:49:35,060
So pretty bad.

1084
00:49:35,060 --> 00:49:36,660
This is a little bit better.

1085
00:49:36,660 --> 00:49:40,980
I've recognized that 355
over 113 is being used twice.

1086
00:49:40,980 --> 00:49:43,050
So I'm saving it as a variable.

1087
00:49:43,050 --> 00:49:47,930
But my variables are
still single characters.

1088
00:49:47,930 --> 00:49:51,530
And my comments are pretty bad.

1089
00:49:51,530 --> 00:49:54,620
I'm basically saying
what the code is doing.

1090
00:49:54,620 --> 00:49:56,670
Please don't do that.

1091
00:49:56,670 --> 00:50:01,410
We can see that a equals
p times r times r.

1092
00:50:01,410 --> 00:50:03,850
I see that I'm multiplying
p with r squared.

1093
00:50:03,850 --> 00:50:07,020
I don't need to read
that in English.

1094
00:50:07,020 --> 00:50:10,590
What I would like to see
is a comment like this.

1095
00:50:10,590 --> 00:50:13,920
Here I'm commenting
a chunk of code.

1096
00:50:13,920 --> 00:50:17,340
And someone who doesn't want
to read this chunk of code

1097
00:50:17,340 --> 00:50:19,020
just reads the
comment, and I already

1098
00:50:19,020 --> 00:50:21,960
know that I'm calculating the
area and circumference using

1099
00:50:21,960 --> 00:50:23,670
an approximation for pi.

1100
00:50:23,670 --> 00:50:27,960
That's a pretty nice comment
there and good descriptive

1101
00:50:27,960 --> 00:50:31,200
names and all that.

1102
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:34,640
So we can actually--
once we create an object,

1103
00:50:34,640 --> 00:50:37,370
a variable-- sorry, once we
create an object and bind it

1104
00:50:37,370 --> 00:50:41,270
to a variable, we can
change the bindings.

1105
00:50:41,270 --> 00:50:43,370
So we can take
that variable name

1106
00:50:43,370 --> 00:50:46,620
and bind it to a
completely different value.

1107
00:50:46,620 --> 00:50:49,880
This might not be
useful right now,

1108
00:50:49,880 --> 00:50:53,000
but it will be useful
when we introduce

1109
00:50:53,000 --> 00:50:55,640
control flow in our programs.

1110
00:50:55,640 --> 00:50:59,390
So to rebind a variable
what that means is

1111
00:50:59,390 --> 00:51:01,640
we're going to take
the name, we're

1112
00:51:01,640 --> 00:51:03,860
going to lose the binding
to the previous value,

1113
00:51:03,860 --> 00:51:05,970
and we're going to
rebind it to a new value.

1114
00:51:05,970 --> 00:51:08,870
So I'm going to show you how
this looks like in memory.

1115
00:51:08,870 --> 00:51:11,660
I'm going to use this
sort of cloud picture

1116
00:51:11,660 --> 00:51:14,750
to represent what
happens behind the scenes

1117
00:51:14,750 --> 00:51:16,040
whenever we write programs.

1118
00:51:16,040 --> 00:51:18,980
And it's like a little animation
to help you understand line

1119
00:51:18,980 --> 00:51:20,760
by line what's going on.

1120
00:51:20,760 --> 00:51:24,080
So here we have pi equals 3.14.

1121
00:51:24,080 --> 00:51:27,920
So the green 3.14 is
my value in memory.

1122
00:51:27,920 --> 00:51:28,700
Cloud is memory.

1123
00:51:28,700 --> 00:51:29,960
That's my value in memory.

1124
00:51:29,960 --> 00:51:32,450
And it's bound to this name pi.

1125
00:51:32,450 --> 00:51:35,440
So this is my variable name.

1126
00:51:35,440 --> 00:51:38,590
The next line, radius
equals 2.2, same thing.

1127
00:51:38,590 --> 00:51:41,740
I've got 2.2 as my value
in memory, my object.

1128
00:51:41,740 --> 00:51:44,930
And radius is the
name for that object.

1129
00:51:44,930 --> 00:51:47,190
Area equals pi times
radius squared.

1130
00:51:47,190 --> 00:51:49,100
So what happens
behind the scenes

1131
00:51:49,100 --> 00:51:51,517
is it calculates this value.

1132
00:51:51,517 --> 00:51:52,850
It doesn't store the expression.

1133
00:51:52,850 --> 00:51:55,550
It stores the value resulting
from the calculation,

1134
00:51:55,550 --> 00:51:58,430
and then it saves it--

1135
00:51:58,430 --> 00:52:01,530
or binds it to the name area.

1136
00:52:01,530 --> 00:52:03,090
OK, everything OK so far?

1137
00:52:03,090 --> 00:52:04,360
We've seen this code before.

1138
00:52:04,360 --> 00:52:04,860
Cool.

1139
00:52:04,860 --> 00:52:06,930
So now what happens
when we do this,

1140
00:52:06,930 --> 00:52:09,720
radius equals radius plus 1?

1141
00:52:09,720 --> 00:52:15,610
In math, that would
say 0 equals 1.

1142
00:52:15,610 --> 00:52:16,840
But we're not in math here.

1143
00:52:16,840 --> 00:52:20,620
We're in computer science,
and this is perfectly valid.

1144
00:52:20,620 --> 00:52:25,180
We're following the rule when
we have an assignment that

1145
00:52:25,180 --> 00:52:29,920
says look at the right-hand
side first and evaluate it

1146
00:52:29,920 --> 00:52:32,570
and then bind it to
the left-hand side.

1147
00:52:32,570 --> 00:52:37,422
So if we look at the right-hand
side first, we see radius.

1148
00:52:37,422 --> 00:52:38,380
Well, what's the value?

1149
00:52:38,380 --> 00:52:40,060
2.2.

1150
00:52:40,060 --> 00:52:43,870
We see add 1 to it, 3.2.

1151
00:52:43,870 --> 00:52:45,610
Save that in memory.

1152
00:52:45,610 --> 00:52:47,840
And then we see the assignment.

1153
00:52:47,840 --> 00:52:52,000
Now save it with
the name radius.

1154
00:52:52,000 --> 00:52:56,260
OK, so we can only have
one variable assigned

1155
00:52:56,260 --> 00:52:58,030
to one value at a time.

1156
00:52:58,030 --> 00:52:59,590
This is not math.

1157
00:52:59,590 --> 00:53:00,850
This is computer science.

1158
00:53:00,850 --> 00:53:05,380
So you can only have radius
point to one thing at a time.

1159
00:53:05,380 --> 00:53:08,590
With this line of code,
radius equals radius plus 1.

1160
00:53:08,590 --> 00:53:12,700
We've lost the binding to
2.2, this object in memory,

1161
00:53:12,700 --> 00:53:17,520
and we've rebound
it to the value 3.2.

1162
00:53:17,520 --> 00:53:19,320
And that's perfectly fine.

1163
00:53:19,320 --> 00:53:21,330
2.2 is now just
sitting in memory.

1164
00:53:21,330 --> 00:53:25,380
We can't get back to it unless
we say maybe radius equals 2.2.

1165
00:53:25,380 --> 00:53:27,480
It just sits in
memory and then might

1166
00:53:27,480 --> 00:53:29,910
be collected later
on by-- or reclaimed

1167
00:53:29,910 --> 00:53:31,840
by garbage collection
or something like that.

1168
00:53:31,840 --> 00:53:34,470
But for now, we
can't get back to it.

1169
00:53:34,470 --> 00:53:39,800
Now, what's the value for area
at the end of these lines?

1170
00:53:39,800 --> 00:53:43,640
Well, according to
this, it's 15.1976.

1171
00:53:43,640 --> 00:53:47,700
So it's using the old
2.2 value for radius.

1172
00:53:47,700 --> 00:53:52,890
And that's OK because
the program never told--

1173
00:53:52,890 --> 00:53:55,950
never had a line that
said recalculate area

1174
00:53:55,950 --> 00:53:59,380
after we changed the radius.

1175
00:53:59,380 --> 00:54:02,830
It's just following,
dumb, line by line.

1176
00:54:02,830 --> 00:54:05,410
It doesn't know that, hey,
if I change the radius,

1177
00:54:05,410 --> 00:54:08,050
the user might want
the area changed.

1178
00:54:08,050 --> 00:54:09,980
It doesn't make
those connections.

1179
00:54:09,980 --> 00:54:12,190
It's just following
instructions.

1180
00:54:12,190 --> 00:54:13,330
And that's OK.

1181
00:54:13,330 --> 00:54:15,350
If we want it to
change the area,

1182
00:54:15,350 --> 00:54:18,400
we would have to copy
this line and paste it

1183
00:54:18,400 --> 00:54:20,020
after we've changed the radius.

1184
00:54:20,020 --> 00:54:24,215
And then the area
would change as well.

1185
00:54:24,215 --> 00:54:25,090
Does that make sense?

1186
00:54:25,090 --> 00:54:28,320
That's kind of an important
part of this lecture.

1187
00:54:28,320 --> 00:54:30,410
OK, cool.

1188
00:54:30,410 --> 00:54:33,493
So big idea here is our
lines are evaluated one

1189
00:54:33,493 --> 00:54:34,160
after the other.

1190
00:54:34,160 --> 00:54:35,060
We're not skipping.

1191
00:54:35,060 --> 00:54:36,500
We're not repeating things.

1192
00:54:36,500 --> 00:54:38,610
That's something we're
going to learn about later.

1193
00:54:38,610 --> 00:54:41,820
But for now, line by line.

1194
00:54:41,820 --> 00:54:44,390
So here's a little you try it.

1195
00:54:44,390 --> 00:54:46,340
These three lines are
executed in order.

1196
00:54:46,340 --> 00:54:49,490
What are the values
for meters and feet

1197
00:54:49,490 --> 00:54:51,230
variables at each line?

1198
00:54:51,230 --> 00:54:54,770
So how about at the first line,
what's the value for meters

1199
00:54:54,770 --> 00:54:57,450
after we execute the first line?

1200
00:54:57,450 --> 00:54:57,950
100.

1201
00:54:57,950 --> 00:54:58,715
What about feet?

1203
00:55:01,620 --> 00:55:05,930
So at the end of the first line,
there is no value for feet yet.

1204
00:55:05,930 --> 00:55:07,730
How about after the second line?

1205
00:55:07,730 --> 00:55:09,320
328.08.

1206
00:55:09,320 --> 00:55:09,980
Right?

1207
00:55:09,980 --> 00:55:11,960
How about the value for meters?

1208
00:55:11,960 --> 00:55:12,680
AUDIENCE: 100.

1209
00:55:12,680 --> 00:55:13,970
ANA BELL: 100 still.

1210
00:55:13,970 --> 00:55:15,950
And what about after
the third line?

1211
00:55:15,950 --> 00:55:20,360
I'm changing meters to 200.

1212
00:55:20,360 --> 00:55:21,380
Exactly, yeah.

1213
00:55:21,380 --> 00:55:26,240
Meters is 200, but
feet is still 328.08.

1214
00:55:26,240 --> 00:55:29,980
And this is something I
want to show you guys today.

1215
00:55:29,980 --> 00:55:31,510
And we're going
to use this Python

1216
00:55:31,510 --> 00:55:34,870
Tutor a lot more in the future.

1217
00:55:34,870 --> 00:55:37,450
Python Tutor is a nice
website that allows

1218
00:55:37,450 --> 00:55:39,460
you to step in your code--

1219
00:55:39,460 --> 00:55:41,600
step through your
code step by step.

1220
00:55:41,600 --> 00:55:44,830
So at each line
that you execute,

1221
00:55:44,830 --> 00:55:47,950
you get to see the values of
