1253
00:57:14,980 --> 00:57:19,000
but only using
commands like this.

1254
00:57:19,000 --> 00:57:22,780
And so the code here is buggy.

1255
00:57:22,780 --> 00:57:23,980
That means it's wrong.

1256
00:57:23,980 --> 00:57:26,650
It has an error in it.

1257
00:57:26,650 --> 00:57:28,270
Well, let's step
through-- let's step

1258
00:57:28,270 --> 00:57:29,710
through a little bit at a time.

1259
00:57:29,710 --> 00:57:32,110
Y equals x.

1260
00:57:32,110 --> 00:57:34,630
What do I do when
y equals x here?

1262
00:57:37,610 --> 00:57:43,110
Yeah, exactly, y is going
to move from 2 to 1.

1263
00:57:43,110 --> 00:57:45,285
Now, what happens
when I do x equals y?

1265
00:57:47,840 --> 00:57:49,820
Yes, x stays the same.

1266
00:57:49,820 --> 00:57:54,260
My first line, y equals
x, lost the binding to 2.

1267
00:57:54,260 --> 00:57:58,470
And now it's all messed up
because I can't get it back.

1268
00:57:58,470 --> 00:58:00,860
So instead-- so if you
didn't understand this,

1269
00:58:00,860 --> 00:58:03,710
you can click Python Tutor
and just step through step

1270
00:58:03,710 --> 00:58:05,510
by step on your own.

1271
00:58:05,510 --> 00:58:07,943
But how can we fix this?

1272
00:58:07,943 --> 00:58:09,755
AUDIENCE: Create
a third variable.

1273
00:58:09,755 --> 00:58:11,600
ANA BELL: Create
a third variable?

1274
00:58:11,600 --> 00:58:12,820
Yeah, that's a great idea.

1275
00:58:12,820 --> 00:58:14,540
Yeah, we can create
a third variable.

1276
00:58:14,540 --> 00:58:16,960
So x is 1, y is 1--

1277
00:58:16,960 --> 00:58:17,863
y is 2.

1278
00:58:17,863 --> 00:58:19,280
So we can create
a third variable.

1279
00:58:19,280 --> 00:58:23,230
What do you want to make
the variable equal to?

1280
00:58:23,230 --> 00:58:25,090
X or y?

1281
00:58:25,090 --> 00:58:27,280
Yeah, either one.

1282
00:58:27,280 --> 00:58:30,190
I made it y, so let's do y.

1283
00:58:30,190 --> 00:58:33,490
So here I've got a temporary
variable called "temp,"

1284
00:58:33,490 --> 00:58:36,970
and I made it equal to 2.

1285
00:58:36,970 --> 00:58:38,395
And now what can I do?

1287
00:58:41,890 --> 00:58:43,410
Which one can I reassign now?

1288
00:58:43,410 --> 00:58:47,390
X equals y, or y equals x?

1289
00:58:47,390 --> 00:58:49,880
Exactly, y equals--
if I do x equals y,

1290
00:58:49,880 --> 00:58:53,430
I lose my binding to 1,
and it messed up again.

1291
00:58:53,430 --> 00:58:55,610
So y equals x is OK to do.

1292
00:58:55,610 --> 00:58:59,290
So I'm going to lose the
binding from y from 2

1293
00:58:59,290 --> 00:59:01,120
and bind it up to 1.

1294
00:59:01,120 --> 00:59:04,590
And now what do I do?

1295
00:59:04,590 --> 00:59:10,960
Yeah, now I can safely
reassign x to temp.

1296
00:59:10,960 --> 00:59:14,830
So I can say x is equal to
temp because temp points to 2.

1297
00:59:14,830 --> 00:59:19,470
And I want to make x
point to 2 as well.

1298
00:59:19,470 --> 00:59:23,297
So in terms of code,
that's sort of the diagram.

1299
00:59:23,297 --> 00:59:24,380
But we can write the code.

1300
00:59:24,380 --> 00:59:27,680
So you don't-- let's see.

1301
00:59:27,680 --> 00:59:31,040
We don't write it in
here, but on your own,

1302
00:59:31,040 --> 00:59:32,828
you can write it in
here if you'd like.

1303
00:59:32,828 --> 00:59:33,870
Or we can do it together.

1304
00:59:33,870 --> 00:59:34,970
So x is equal--

1305
00:59:34,970 --> 00:59:35,480
oops.

1306
00:59:35,480 --> 00:59:39,050
X equals 1, y equals 2.

1307
00:59:39,050 --> 00:59:41,780
And then we had temp.

1308
00:59:41,780 --> 00:59:45,020
We wanted to assign
it to whatever y was.

1309
00:59:45,020 --> 00:59:48,120
So we say temp is equal to y.

1310
00:59:48,120 --> 00:59:50,640
And if you want to check
the values of the variables,

1311
00:59:50,640 --> 00:59:52,350
you can just invoke the names.

1312
00:59:52,350 --> 00:59:59,380
So x is 1, y is 2, and temp
should be whatever y is, 2.

1313
00:59:59,380 --> 01:00:00,980
OK, good so far.

1314
01:00:00,980 --> 01:00:07,210
So now I'm at the step
here, I think, right?

1315
01:00:07,210 --> 01:00:08,480
I've just created this.

1316
01:00:08,480 --> 01:00:10,120
And then the last
thing I need to do

1317
01:00:10,120 --> 01:00:15,730
is lose the binding from
x to whatever temp is.

1318
01:00:15,730 --> 01:00:18,320
So I want to do
this operation here,

1319
01:00:18,320 --> 01:00:23,540
which means I want to assign
x to be equal to temp.

1320
01:00:23,540 --> 01:00:26,960
So now x is 2, y is 1.

1322
01:00:29,720 --> 01:00:32,850
What did I do?

1323
01:00:32,850 --> 01:00:36,120
Yeah, so this happens sometimes.

1324
01:00:36,120 --> 01:00:39,210
We can just start all over.

1325
01:00:39,210 --> 01:00:41,670
So y equals temp.

1326
01:00:41,670 --> 01:00:42,540
Sorry.

1327
01:00:42,540 --> 01:00:44,910
Temp equals y.

1329
01:00:49,610 --> 01:00:52,360
Y equals x.

1330
01:00:52,360 --> 01:00:53,620
Y is one.

1331
01:00:53,620 --> 01:00:54,670
X is 1.

1332
01:00:54,670 --> 01:00:58,060
And then x equals temp.

1333
01:00:58,060 --> 01:01:00,940
Y is one, x is 2.

1334
01:01:00,940 --> 01:01:04,480
So it's OK if things go wrong.

1335
01:01:04,480 --> 01:01:05,870
They will go wrong.

1336
01:01:05,870 --> 01:01:08,080
We can just start all over
in this particular case

1337
01:01:08,080 --> 01:01:10,300
by redefining our
variables and just

1338
01:01:10,300 --> 01:01:11,960
trying it out all over again.

1339
01:01:11,960 --> 01:01:13,730
So that's kind of
what the shell is for.

1340
01:01:13,730 --> 01:01:14,660
That's what I use it for.

1341
01:01:14,660 --> 01:01:16,827
That's what we're going to
use it for in the future,

1342
01:01:16,827 --> 01:01:20,950
just to do quick things
like this and also things

1343
01:01:20,950 --> 01:01:24,070
like checking the types
and other commands we've

1344
01:01:24,070 --> 01:01:27,700
done earlier.

1345
01:01:27,700 --> 01:01:30,910
OK, so any questions
before we do the summary?

1346
01:01:30,910 --> 01:01:33,640
Was this all right pace
or was it too fast?

1347
01:01:33,640 --> 01:01:35,510
Or it was OK?

1348
01:01:35,510 --> 01:01:36,700
OK, good.

1349
01:01:36,700 --> 01:01:37,700
Thumbs up is good.

1350
01:01:37,700 --> 01:01:40,280
So let's do a quick summary.

1351
01:01:40,280 --> 01:01:43,420
We saw that we can
create programs

1352
01:01:43,420 --> 01:01:45,160
by manipulating objects.

1353
01:01:45,160 --> 01:01:47,500
We created objects in Python.

1354
01:01:47,500 --> 01:01:50,080
And we saw that objects
have a particular type.

1355
01:01:50,080 --> 01:01:53,470
The type that the object
has tells Python the things

1356
01:01:53,470 --> 01:01:57,260
that you can do
with that object.

1357
01:01:57,260 --> 01:02:00,770
We can combine objects
in expressions.

1358
01:02:00,770 --> 01:02:04,000
And these expressions
evaluate or boil down

1359
01:02:04,000 --> 01:02:06,310
to one particular value.

1360
01:02:06,310 --> 01:02:09,850
Objects or values can
be stored in variables.

1361
01:02:09,850 --> 01:02:13,510
And these variables allow
us to access these values

1362
01:02:13,510 --> 01:02:15,820
with nicer names later
on in our program.

1363
01:02:15,820 --> 01:02:20,470
And then we're able to write
neater, more legible programs

1364
01:02:20,470 --> 01:02:21,790
as well.

1365
01:02:21,790 --> 01:02:22,600
So the equal sign--

1366
01:02:22,600 --> 01:02:25,360
I showed you a couple of
differences between math

1367
01:02:25,360 --> 01:02:26,290
and computer science.

1368
01:02:26,290 --> 01:02:29,840
The equal sign was one
notable difference.

1369
01:02:29,840 --> 01:02:31,910
The equal sign in
math is declarative,

1370
01:02:31,910 --> 01:02:34,910
and the equal sign in computer
science is an assignment.

1371
01:02:34,910 --> 01:02:38,600
You're basically saying this
is associated with this.

1372
01:02:38,600 --> 01:02:44,230
And we're not doing any sort of
equality in computer science.

1373
01:02:44,230 --> 01:02:48,640
And yes, computers do
what you tell them to do.

1374
01:02:48,640 --> 01:02:51,340
That's kind of the
big thing here.

1375
01:02:51,340 --> 01:02:54,010
Line by line, it executes
starting from the top,

1376
01:02:54,010 --> 01:02:55,880
goes line by line.

1377
01:02:55,880 --> 01:02:58,960
So far, we haven't
seen any places where

1378
01:02:58,960 --> 01:03:00,610
the computer makes a decision.

1379
01:03:00,610 --> 01:03:02,560
But next lecture,
we will see how

1380
01:03:02,560 --> 01:03:06,130
we can insert decision
points in our programs

1381
01:03:06,130 --> 01:03:10,420
for the computer to either
execute one set of code

1382
01:03:10,420 --> 01:03:12,380
or another set of code.

1383
01:03:12,380 --> 01:03:14,870
All right, so that's the
end of today's lecture.

1384
01:03:14,870 --> 01:03:16,070
Thank you all for joining.

1385
01:03:16,070 --> 01:03:18,330
I will see you on Monday.